{\\text{new}}^{′})\\,Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.5{x}^{2}-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+0.5{x}^{2}-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5{x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-0.5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Then the function $g$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot {2}^{2}-0.5\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-3}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.600\\,00 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.466\\,116 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.456\\,216\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈1.46,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535537 true] [17592186354284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354285 13194139535537 true] [17592186354284 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535537 true] [17592186354284 1041 "field-1" 13194139535537 true] [17592186354284 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354285 13194139535537 true] [17592186354284 1110 #uuid "6d064b82-c9d7-537f-9e41-44c0a81f6906" 13194139535537 true] [17592186354285 1034 "1.46" 13194139535537 true] [17592186354285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535537 true] [17592186354285 1111 #uuid "580d28f6-59b0-5f7b-b3c0-ef57313a6b98" 13194139535537 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-334072" 17592186354260 "q-334072/selection" 17592186354261 "q-334072/selection/05320af95aebb9d3f457411af62e3da824a12d4d4a6641f1d48b4f1504e79ef2" 17592186354262 "q-334072/selection/0a4731710025ba10eeed887b2293e48fb5c0b84aee3c9c5079fda0c4fef0f9fc" 17592186354263 "q-334072/selection/1df72a42ae6ce0eeb848cc612ec18ad01b5266e34a7ae355a0e347c54077d99d" 17592186354264 "q-334072/selection/6921b2661240705c5c69abae30eb94c0a625c81320cdca52fca8b0efeea58785" 17592186354265 "q-334072/selection/7d598dfecc69dce021edd49b85c54eb925e4a47c4fdb6109092043e30d794402" 17592186354266 "q-334077" 17592186354267 "q-334077/selection" 17592186354268 "q-334077/selection/3b808c285da235dcaf0a5d33ccc5d5a52d9f4da2498e15c00ac81d5dd3252848" 17592186354269 "q-334077/selection/4e3a08993c651ef656d2b7ed775d9f747ff607a109625bc1c046324ae62a8530" 17592186354270 "q-334077/selection/a763ceec3273e947ce5bbcdcc00e7a1554e5d8169740cda54107453607d968b5" 17592186354271 "q-334077/selection/a93ba937a59d3514c1a34cf0b794b2836d29b8ac4af78ac2f97615ef69cd59d2" 17592